window.docContents[23].resolve({"/Introduction/#PDEAtlas--Introduction--Formalization-in-Lean":{"contents":"\n\nFor the sine mode, \"the boundary term vanishes\" can be checked by evaluating\nthe sine at zero and . For a weak solution in a zero-boundary Sobolev\nspace, the same words refer to approximation or traces. The diagram shows\nwhy moving between solution concepts needs a mathematical argument: an\nintegration-by-parts step moves in one direction, a regularity theorem may\npermit the reverse direction, and minimizing an energy introduces its own\nconditions.\n\nIn several variables the Fréchet derivative is a continuous linear map; applying it to a coordinate\ndirection gives a partial derivative. In one dimension we use the ordinary\nscalar derivative. Building these operations from Mathlib's definitions lets\na coordinate calculation feed into theorems about linear maps and norms.\nThe mathlib4 discussion\nPartial derivatives\nexplains why coordinates, differentiation along a line, and Fréchet\ndifferentiation need to be distinguished. Regularity hypotheses tell us\nwhen we may move between them.\n\nThere is a less familiar consequence of using these definitions. Lean\nfunctions are total: division, logarithms, square roots, and differentiation\nreturn values even outside the domain where a textbook uses their usual\nrules. We can write  without first proving that  is twice\ndifferentiable. To interpret it as a classical Laplacian, we must supply the\nregularity that justifies the two differentiations. Similarly, the rational\nidentity in the opening notebook keeps its nonzero-denominator assumption.\nThe ability to write an expression does not grant the calculus rule we want\nto apply to it.\n\nEquality needs comparable care. A function can change on a set of measure\nzero without changing its  class or the distribution it represents.\nSuch a change can still alter a pointwise boundary value. To recover a\npointwise statement from an almost-everywhere one, we need further structure,\nsuch as continuous representatives. The time-continuity theorem for weak\nheat flow is one example of this work, not a matter of changing notation.\n\nBoundary regularity presents another choice. \"Let  be smooth\" can\nmean many things depending on the theorem: extension, trace, surface measure,\nGreen's identity, elliptic estimates, and boundary flattening need different\ngeometric assumptions. Where a proof uses a chart, a cutoff, a flattening\nmap, a Jacobian estimate, or an orientation, PDEAtlas can take that object\nand its properties as explicit input. This makes a local analytic argument\nusable before every geometric construction is available. It also leaves an\nobligation visible: a theorem assuming those data is not yet a construction\nof them from a Lipschitz or smooth boundary.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Introduction","header":"1.4. Formalization in Lean","id":"/Introduction/#PDEAtlas--Introduction--Formalization-in-Lean"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Classical-and-Weak-Heat-Flow--Improved-regularity":{"contents":"The  theorems take an autonomous symmetric, uniformly elliptic principal\npart without lower-order terms, with  coefficients\nadmitting global  representatives, on a domain ,\n, carrying a twice differentiable boundary atlas. Data  and\n give  and\n (). Forcing in\n and compatible initial data in  give the\nprincipal-part case of Evans's Section 7.1.3, Theorem 5(ii), with data-only\nbounds (). Regularity of every\norder and a unique classical solution smooth up to the boundary, Theorems 6\nand 7, hold for smooth time-independent coefficients, lower-order terms\nincluded, on bounded domains whose boundary is  for every \n( and\n). The open directions closest\nto these results are general measure-valued initial data, nonhomogeneous and\nmixed boundary conditions, and  estimates for time-dependent\ncoefficients.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Classical and Weak Heat Flow","header":"6.8.1.4. Improved regularity","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Classical-and-Weak-Heat-Flow--Improved-regularity"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--Sobolev___-Morrey___-Poincar______-and-Hardy-Inequalities--The-exponent-trichotomy--The-subcritical-range-1-___-p-_LT_-n":{"contents":"The Sobolev conjugate is\n\n\n\nThe global Gagliardo–Nirenberg–Sobolev estimate controls the\n norm by the first derivative for compactly supported smooth\nfunctions, and density carries it to the completed space. At order ,\nrepeated first-order steps give\n\n\n\nas long as .  Each step consumes one derivative and replaces the\ncurrent exponent by its first-order Sobolev conjugate.  The proof uses\nderivative-word slices of the graph bundle, which record which weak\nderivative becomes the base coordinate of the next first-order Sobolev\nelement.\n\nOn a domain there are two routes: a supplied order- extension operator\ntransfers the whole-space theorem, or an operational bounded  atlas\nperforms the iteration directly through first-order slices. Both give bounded\noperators.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Extension, Trace, and Embeddings\u0009Sobolev, Morrey, Poincaré, and Hardy Inequalities\u0009The exponent trichotomy","header":"4.3.4.1.1. The subcritical range 1 ≤ p < n","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--Sobolev___-Morrey___-Poincar______-and-Hardy-Inequalities--The-exponent-trichotomy--The-subcritical-range-1-___-p-_LT_-n"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Scope___-Trust___-and-Notes/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Scope___-Trust___-and-Notes--Historical-notes-and-references":{"contents":"Proof-producing computation belongs to a longer line of work. Conventional\ncomputer algebra systems optimize for breadth and speed; proof assistants ask\nfor an additional object, a proposition checked by the kernel, before a result\ncan be reused as a theorem. Interval arithmetic, polynomial approximation,\nexact real arithmetic, root counting, and certified quadrature have been\nformalized in proof assistants such as HOL Light, Coq, and Isabelle.\n\nSymbolic integration.  and\n describe the theory of elementary\nintegration, including the Risch decision procedure and the Rothstein–Trager\nalgorithm. The checked search here covers bounded families of primitives and\nverifies every candidate by differentiation.\n\nTaylor models and validated numerics.  and\n provide background on Taylor models and\ntheir use in validated computation, including Taylor-model arithmetic with\nwrapping control. The Taylor-model certificates here are approximation and\ncomposition theorems for a finite polynomial with a uniform remainder.\n\nInterval arithmetic.  and\n provide general\nbackground on interval methods. We use exact rational intervals and\nproved enclosure theorems for the supported arithmetic and transcendental\noperations.\n\nModern computer arithmetic.  is a general\nreference for exact and multiple-precision arithmetic. Here the trusted\nstatements are the Lean theorems attached to the concrete arithmetic\noperations.\n\nSturm's theorem and root isolation. Starting from the real-root count of\n, the polynomial layer contains checked Sturm\nrecurrences, root counts, and root-isolation certificates, including the global\nisolator of the exact section.\n and  are useful\ncomparisons for formal real-algebraic reasoning.\n\nFormal verification of arithmetic. ,\n, ,\nand  provide background on reflection\nand proved numerical bounds in proof assistants. We follow the same\nchecked-computation pattern for the expression and\ncertificate types.\n\nLean and Mathlib.  describes the\nproof assistant, and  its mathematical library.\nThey supply the type theory, analytic definitions, and reusable proofs used\nthroughout this development.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Scope, Trust, and Notes","header":"10.10.3. Historical notes and references","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Scope___-Trust___-and-Notes/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Scope___-Trust___-and-Notes--Historical-notes-and-references"}});